{
  "name": "Linguistics - BA",
  "kind": "major",
  "type": "Bachelor's Degree",
  "department": "Linguistics",
  "source": "https://catalog.ucsd.edu/curric/LING-ug.html",
  "narrative": [
    "Every linguistics major (except the language studies major) must satisfy the undergraduate language requirement and must successfully complete a minimum of twelve upper-division courses. The general major requires twelve upper-division courses.",
    "Except for LIGN 188R, LIGN 195, LIGN 199, and LIGN 199H, no course taken on a Pass/Not Pass basis may be counted toward a linguistics major. No more than four combined units of LIGN 188R, LIGN 195, and LIGN 199 may be counted toward a linguistics major. An additional four units of LIGN 199H may be counted toward a linguistics major.",
    "A letter grade of C– or better is required for every course counted toward a linguistics major, including courses taken to satisfy the department’s undergraduate language requirement.",
    "LIGN 87 and LIGN 192 do not count as linguistics electives (restricted courses).",
    "At least six out of the twelve upper-division linguistics courses counted toward the major must be linguistics courses taken in residence at UC San Diego.",
    "Independent study (LIGN 199) requires at least a 3.5 GPA in the major courses; no more than one such course (taken P/NP) may count toward the major."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Undergraduate Language Requirement",
      "requirement": {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "All linguistics majors must demonstrate proficiency in one non-English natural language by one of the following: By passing the reading proficiency examination and the oral interview administered by the Department of Linguistics in French, German, Italian, Russian, Spanish, Arabic, Farsi, Tagalog, Portuguese, Vietnamese, or American Sign Language; or By successfully completing a course given at UC San Diego representing the fourth quarter (or beyond) of instruction in any single non-English natural language with a grade of C– or better; or By scoring four or greater on the Advanced Placement (AP) exam in a non-English natural language. Students with native language competence in a language other than English may petition to have English count as satisfying the proficiency requirement."
      }
    },
